      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-183366",
      ":question/problem": "Use mathematical induction to prove that ${2}^{n+1}>n+1$ for $n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-2}}={{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is\n{{field-4}}\nthe right-hand side.\nThus, our statement is {{field-5}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-6}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}>{{field-8}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-9}} & ={{field-10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by the inductive hypothesis, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {2}^{k+1} & >{{field-11}} \\\\  & ={{field-12}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & >{{field-13}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${2}^{n+1}>n+1$ is true for all\n{{field-14}}."
